WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.770
All right, next, an incredible story

2
00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:04.470
about how even the most difficult times

3
00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.470
can bring out incredible good in people.

4
00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.250
It's about two college kids who came together

5
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.740
to help families a world away.

6
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.800 line:15%
The Washington Post reports that Harvard students

7
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.940 line:15%
Avi Schiffmann and Marco Burstein

8
00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:22.460 line:15%
joined forces to build a website in just three days

9
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.900 line:15%
to help Ukrainian refugees and find places to live.

10
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.480 line:15%
It's called Ukrainian Take Shelter,

11
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.450 line:15%
and it functions kind of like Airbnb.

12
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.860 line:15%
If you have a house, apartment, a room,

13
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.450 line:15%
even a couch to share, you can post it.

14
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.150 line:15%
It doesn't matter where you live.

15
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.610 line:15%
People are offering spaces in Poland, Romania, Canada,

16
00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.740 line:15%
the US, with some hosts even offering

17
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.320 line:15%
to cover airfare to get them there.

18
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.520 line:15%
And refugees can come with pets.

19
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.450 line:15%
Some have, you know, their pets with them.

20
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:53.980 line:15%
Avi and Marco built this site

21
00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.470 line:15%
to operate in many languages, also, which is so clever.

22
00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:00.300 line:15%
Polish, Ukrainian, Czech, and English.

23
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.310 line:15%
It's so usable, they set it up literally within days,

24
00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.430
and for more information on this,

25
00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.430 line:15%
you can go to our website at thedrewbarrymoreshow.com

26
00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.314 line:15%
to get information on that.

27
00:01:11.314 --> 00:01:15.130 line:15%
Yeah.
(audience applauds)

28
00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:16.943
And it's so good.

29
00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:19.320
It's good, you know, of course there are

30
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.800
really tough things going on, we all know that,

31
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.600
but the are moments like this, things like this,

32
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.160
that remind me, remind us all,

33
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.030
of the good that comes out of this,

34
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.740
about human decency and kindness and coming together,

35
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.400
so well done you two.

36
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.340
Very inspired.
Yeah, those boys

37
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.290
just have my heart.

38
00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.780
I saw that story and I just wanted to relay it

39
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.470
as soon as possible.
Yeah. I love it.

40
00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.380
Well, next up, we've got an incredible story

41
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.270
about two single moms joining forces to buy their own home.

42
00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:46.103
Listen to this.

43
00:01:46.103 --> 00:01:48.810 line:15%
In her essay on Insider, single mom Holly Harper

44
00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.610 line:15%
shares the story that she and her friend, Herrin Hopper,

45
00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.350 line:15%
another single mom, were divorced and looking

46
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.230
for some housing stability and they couldn't do it alone.

47
00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.030
So they formed a partnership

48
00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:02.150
and bought the perfect multi-unit home together.

49
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.960 line:15%
Then they rented out the additional units to two more women,

50
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.940 line:15%
so now the house includes, okay, ready?

51
00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:10.160 line:15%
Five kids, three dogs, two hamsters, and a gecko,

52
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.810 line:15%
and they say this built in community has been great

53
00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.700 line:15%
for the kids because they meet a ton of neighborhood people,

54
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.430 line:15%
lots of support with childcare, carpools, dog walking.

55
00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:24.150 line:15%
This is another story about people coming together

56
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.530 line:15%
when you just need humankind to take care of one another.

57
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:31.260 line:15%
I mean, as someone who became a single mom,

58
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.010
this story just really spoke to me.

59
00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.870
I was desperate for community.

60
00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.940
I moved from Los Angeles to New York,

61
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.030
I felt very alone,

62
00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:45.710
and I think I've always made jokes with my friends

63
00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:48.120
about like, "One day shouldn't we just all live

64
00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.840
on a commune anyway?"

65
00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.600
Yeah, yeah, come on.
I think safety

66
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.900
is in numbers, I think this is such a brilliant story.

67
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.750
I kind of hope that it's a spark that ignites a movement.

68
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.210
I am so moved by this story.

69
00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.640
Yeah, and it shows that you can define

70
00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.330
what your family is, right?

71
00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:06.530
There's no mold to fit.

72
00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:09.310
And if we can take care of each other in any way possible,

73
00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:10.143
we should do it.

74
00:03:10.143 --> 00:03:12.360
I just am wondering how many bathrooms are in this house,

75
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.280
because there are a lot of people there.

76
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.687
That's a good point, Rossy.
Thank you so much.

77
00:03:17.687 --> 00:03:20.270 line:15%
(upbeat music)

